# Build output
/build/
/build_cuda/
/build_cuda2/
/build_het/
/build_verify/
/build_asan/
/build_plain/
/build_prof/
# CPU-only tree (scraps/build_cpu.bat, configured with an empty CMAKE_CUDA_COMPILER).
# Exists so mode-W work can be built and tested while a long loom render holds
# build_cuda2/bin/ftrace.exe open and blocks relinking it.
/build_cpu/
/out/
# ftrace.exe copied into the repo root by build.bat (build artifact). A RelWithDebInfo
# binary copied there for debugging drags its .pdb along, so ignore that too.
/ftrace.exe
/ftrace.pdb
*.ppm
*.exr
# `-hdr` writes a float sidecar next to every -o; it is a render output like the PNG it
# accompanies, and at 3 floats/px it is the biggest of them.
*.pfm
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.mp4
*.webp
# ...but the small image ASSETS the sample scenes reference are inputs, not render
# outputs, and must be committed or scenes/*.ftsl won't load for anyone else.
!/scenes/*.ppm
# textures/ holds prepared cap textures (tools/make_marble_caps.py), which gallery_rain
# loads by name — without them the scene fails to load. They are <=1024 px and ~3.5 MB
# total. The RAW `marble texture *.{jpg,jpeg,avif}` drops they are built from stay
# untracked: they are source material of unverified provenance (one is a watermarked
# stock comp — see known-issues.md), and the prepared PNGs are the actual asset.
!/textures/*.png
# The README's demo clip. It IS a rendered output like every other .mp4/.gif here, but it
# is also the only moving picture of what ftrace does that anyone landing on the repo page
# will see, so it has to live in the repo rather than merely be regenerable from it
# (`python tools/loom/examples/pastel_jack.py --render --name pastel_jack_ring`).
#
# BOTH forms are committed on purpose. GitHub's Markdown sanitiser deletes <video> tags
# outright, and raw.githubusercontent serves a repo .mp4 as `application/octet-stream`
# with `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, so no browser will play it inline — an animated
# GIF is the only thing that renders on the repo page. The GIF is therefore the embed
# (320x320 / 20 fps / 3.3 MB, gifski q65) and the MP4 is the full-quality download
# (480x480 / 60 fps / 3.4 MB). Together ~6.6 MB, small enough not to bloat a clone.
!/pastel_jack_ring.mp4
!/pastel_jack_ring.gif
# Same reasoning for the README's second demo, the STILL of the `fur` generator: a render
# output like every other .png here, but also the only picture of the coat anyone landing
# on the repo page will see, so it has to be IN the repo rather than merely regenerable
# (`ftrace -in scenes/fur_creature.ftsl -mode D -noise 2`). 720x540, ~0.9 MB.
!/fur_creature_gi.png
# The demo's SCENE, by contrast, is NOT committed in rendered form: the animation is
# tools/loom/examples/pastel_jack.py, and the ~1700 per-frame `.ftsl` it emits into png/
# are generated artifacts exactly like the PNGs beside them. `.ftsl` has no blanket rule
# (scenes/*.ftsl are hand-written source), so ignore the emitted ones by location. This
# also retires png/_objseq_test_frames/scenes/frame_0000*.ftsl, five emitted frames that
# had been swept into a commit before any rule covered them; nothing references them.
/png/**/*.ftsl

# 3D model assets (large binaries kept on disk, never committed — cameras/, meshes/,
# Meshy_* textures, etc. Scenes reference them locally; they don't belong in history.)
*.glb
*.gltf
*.usdz
*.usda
*.zip

# Render state / checkpoints / photon maps (crash-safe sidecars, regenerated on render)
*.ftbuf
*.ftpmap

# Render logs
*.log

# Cached web reference dumps (POV-Wiki pages saved with "... - POV-Wiki_files/" dirs)
*POV-Wiki.html
*POV-Wiki_files/

# Generated meshes (regenerate with tools/make_mesh.py)
*.obj
# ...except this one: a hand-written UV'd unit quad that a shipped scene
# (scenes/emit_pattern.ftsl) depends on, not a generated mesh.
!meshes/uvquad.obj
# Binary meshes (loom.ftmesh). Always generated -- the live viewer channel writes one
# per frame into its scratch dir; nothing checked in depends on one existing.
*.ftmesh

# Reference PDFs (e.g. Kodak Wratten handbook used to digitize filter data)
*.pdf

# Checkpoints / render state
*.ckpt
/renders/

# tools/gpda_lexcheck build output (rebuild with its build.bat)
/tools/gpda_lexcheck/obj/
/tools/gpda_lexcheck/lexcheck.exe

# Scratch / throwaway scripts and test outputs
/scraps/
/tools/loom/scraps/

# Run artifacts of the loom examples: only the `.py` is source. Running one emits an
# `.ftsl` (plus `.obj` mesh sidecars, already covered above) and, for a viewable
# `build()` example, a multi-megabyte introspection `.json`. All regenerable.
/tools/loom/examples/*.ftsl
/tools/loom/examples/*.json

# Raw upstream data dumps (kept locally; derived + attributed CSVs live in data/)
/data_raw/

# Cached POV-Ray source fetched by tools/pov_*_gen.py (AGPL; re-fetched on demand)
/tools/.pov_fnintern.cpp
/tools/.pov_noise.cpp
/tools/.pov_portablenoise.cpp

# Python bytecode cache
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Editor / IDE
.vs/
.vscode/
*.user

# Agent runtime
.claude/

# OS
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
/build_nocuda/

# built on demand by tools/jsonbench.bat (source is checked in, binary is not)
tools/jsonbench.exe
# built on demand by tools/ftslbench.bat (source is checked in, binary is not)
tools/ftslbench.exe

# built on demand by tools/meshbench.bat (source is checked in, binary is not)
tools/meshbench.exe

# MuJoCo writes this next to the cwd whenever a warning fires; pure noise.
MUJOCO_LOG.TXT
